# Yosys synthesis script for Xilinx Kintex-7 K325T (Genesys 2, ffg900).
# Invoked by fpga/run_synthesis_xc7.sh and the fpga-bitstream CI job.
#
# Top is thiele_cpu_top_genesys2 — a board-specific outer wrapper that takes
# the Genesys 2's 200MHz LVDS sysclk, converts it via IBUFDS, and feeds the
# canonical thiele_cpu_top (from thiele_cpu_top_min.v) which in turn ties off
# mkModule1's 250+ Bluespec method I/Os internally. Only 5 board pins survive
# (LVDS clk_p/clk_n, cpu_reset_n, 3 LEDs).
#
# Target: xc7k325tffg900-2 (840 DSP48E1 + 203,800 LUT6) — the Digilent Genesys
# 2 board.
#
# DSP inference is off (`-nodsp`). `instr_chsh_lassert`'s
# `column_contractive_check_witness` performs 22 wide integer multiplications.
# In Kami the check is a 29-phase FSM (`chsh_lassert_fsm` rule in
# coq/kami_hw/ThieleCPUCore.v) that time-shares one 67×67 multiplier, so only
# one multiply is live per cycle. Every register in the FSM is sized to the
# largest value 32-bit counters can produce there (67, 134 and 268 bits), and
# the two 134×134 products are each summed from four 67×67 partial products.
# Built from LUTs, the whole design synthesises to about 37K LUTs.
#
# The same FSM with every value carried at roughly twice that width (128-,
# 256- and 384-bit registers) came to about 69K LUTs, and nextpnr-xilinx
# could not finish routing it inside the CI job's limit. Mapping the
# multiplier onto DSP48E1 slices did not help: cascaded slices stalled the
# analytical placer, and standalone slices left routing badly congested.
# DSP vs LUT is a silicon-utilisation choice, not a correctness one. The
# proof chain (Coq → OCaml → Bluespec → Verilog) is identical either way.
#
# `-abc9` enables abc9 tech-mapping for tighter wide-mux factoring.

read_verilog -sv -DSYNTHESIS RegFile.v thiele_cpu_kami.v thiele_cpu_top_min.v thiele_cpu_top_genesys2.v

synth_xilinx -family xc7 -flatten -abc9 -nodsp -top thiele_cpu_top_genesys2
write_json ../../../build/thiele_xc7k325t.json
